## Reviewer notes on the final fix

- **Core change:** Single-email-per-`sendEmail` call (one invocation per Opportunity) is the root cause of `Too many Email Invocations: 11`. Salesforce limits the number of `Messaging.sendEmail` calls per transaction (documented as 10). Sending one email per Opportunity in a 200-Opportunity run produces 200 invocations.
- **Solution:** Build all `SingleEmailMessage` objects up front (construction is free of governor limits), then batch them into `Messaging.sendEmail` calls of 20. For a 200-row Monday run that is exactly 10 invocations — within the limit — and each batch is independently try/caught so one failure doesn’t abort the rest, preserving the “one email fails, rest still go out” requirement as closely as the governor model allows.
- **`EMAIL_BATCH_SIZE = 20`:** Chosen specifically so `ceil(200/20) = 10` invocations, hitting but not exceeding Salesforce’s documented invocation cap. It is also safely below the per-call message-array size cap in every API version.
- **Queue-owner safety:** Added `AND Owner.UserRoleId != NULL` to the SOQL so Opportunities owned by a Queue (which has no User record) are skipped rather than throwing in `setTargetObjectId`.
- **No reserved words / API misuse:** Removed the stray `end` token and the nonexistent `getRange` method; batches are built with a plain indexed loop.
- **Test hygiene:** The test inserts 200 Opportunities, invokes the service, and asserts that the call returns cleanly instead of throwing `System.LimitException`. Empty/null input cases are also covered.

